{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e","id":1550682,"title":"Aberrant 1e vs 2e","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-05-05T05:20:14-07:00","id":1550682,"title":"Aberrant 1e vs 2e","content":"I've been playing World of Darkness since '96 but have never dipped my toes into any of the other WW/OP properties other than a very brief game of Exalted.<br /> With evolving tastes, I fancied trying something different and Aberrant piqued my interest.<br /> The trouble is, I can't decide on which edition to go for.<br /> <br /> Please could you fine folks list the pros and cons of both editions, and &quot;sell me&quot; on your preferred one.<br /> I'm a complete noob to the Trinity setting, so please treat me as such \ud83d\ude06","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-05T06:51:06-07:00","id":1550684,"title":"","content":"I prefer 2e, but that\u2019s mostly because it ties in so well with the rest of the Trinity Continuum, which I really like. Also, 1e has a much more apocalyptic feel, where 2e is more like a snapshot of the height of the Nova Age. Hope this helps.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-05T10:36:34-07:00","id":1550697,"title":"","content":"Elaborating on this: in 1e, the Aberrants were a movement of Novas who had stumbled across a conspiracy to suppress Novas, and who went underground to fight it. In 2e, the Aberrants are an alternate name for the Novas who consider themselves to be beyond humanity in terms of morality as well as power. This difference means that 2e lacks the conspiracy to suppress Novas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-05T18:17:17-07:00","id":1550713,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't say there's no conspiracy against Novas... just not as a primary antagonist.<br /> <br /> Broadly speaking:<br /> <br /> 2e kinda gets all the pros for me.<br /> <br /> Mechanically 2e has some flaws, but is a bunch better base rule set for the style of game. 1e has bigger more glaring flaws (but is not unplayable by any means) and strains under being a more classic WoD base without a lot of changes that have come from the design experiences that Exalted and Scion 1e provided.<br /> <br /> The &quot;default&quot; setting of 2e is a lot easier to either run as its own stand alone thing or part of a a single shared time-line with the other Trinity Continuum games (which was an explicit point, but also why the whole conspiracy thing isn't as prominent because the TC doesn't want people to be forced into a specific order of events that transition between the different eras). It's also just a take on super powered people that's more in line with audiences in the 2020s; including I think a good section on discussing the different comic book eras and how to incorporate their themes and styles into Aberrant. While shows like The Boys and Invincible certainly show that there's a taste for the darker tone of 1e in modern audiences, it's still very much a product of the Dark/Iron Age of comics (and oWW's places as a maker of &quot;dark and edgy&quot; games back in the 90s). <br /> <br /> So, yeah, better (if worth looking at Storypath Ultra for some fixes) rules and a default style that expands on the original without losing touch of its starting point is kind of a simple winner. It's basically what you'd want out of a new edition even if it wasn't such a big separation of time and a large rules change.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T12:18:40-07:00","id":1550749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550684#post1550684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I prefer 2e, but that\u2019s mostly because it ties in so well with the rest of the Trinity Continuum, which I really like. Also, 1e has a much more apocalyptic feel, where 2e is more like a snapshot of the height of the Nova Age. Hope this helps.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you.<br /> I have to admit, my tastes have changed over the years. A few years ago I would have probably wanted to run a darker game, but right now I'm interested in more of a homage to the 90s X-Men animated series. 2nd edition seems like it would support that better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T12:20:09-07:00","id":1550750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550697#post1550697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Elaborating on this: in 1e, the Aberrants were a movement of Novas who had stumbled across a conspiracy to suppress Novas, and who went underground to fight it. In 2e, the Aberrants are an alternate name for the Novas who consider themselves to be beyond humanity in terms of morality as well as power. This difference means that 2e lacks the conspiracy to suppress Novas.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool. Thank you.<br /> I think I'd probably prefer there not to be a conspiracy in my games. Leaning towards 2e it seems.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T12:26:37-07:00","id":1550752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550713#post1550713\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wouldn't say there's no conspiracy against Novas... just not as a primary antagonist.<br /> <br /> Broadly speaking:<br /> <br /> 2e kinda gets all the pros for me.<br /> <br /> Mechanically 2e has some flaws, but is a bunch better base rule set for the style of game. 1e has bigger more glaring flaws (but is not unplayable by any means) and strains under being a more classic WoD base without a lot of changes that have come from the design experiences that Exalted and Scion 1e provided.<br /> <br /> The &quot;default&quot; setting of 2e is a lot easier to either run as its own stand alone thing or part of a a single shared time-line with the other Trinity Continuum games (which was an explicit point, but also why the whole conspiracy thing isn't as prominent because the TC doesn't want people to be forced into a specific order of events that transition between the different eras). It's also just a take on super powered people that's more in line with audiences in the 2020s; including I think a good section on discussing the different comic book eras and how to incorporate their themes and styles into Aberrant. While shows like The Boys and Invincible certainly show that there's a taste for the darker tone of 1e in modern audiences, it's still very much a product of the Dark/Iron Age of comics (and oWW's places as a maker of &quot;dark and edgy&quot; games back in the 90s).<br /> <br /> So, yeah, better (if worth looking at Storypath Ultra for some fixes) rules and a default style that expands on the original without losing touch of its starting point is kind of a simple winner. It's basically what you'd want out of a new edition even if it wasn't such a big separation of time and a large rules change.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for a brilliant summation.<br /> It sounds like 2e would be my best bet.<br /> Not to bore you with my plans, but I was hoping to run something more in the lines of a homage to the 90s X-Men animated series, and 2e sounds like it would handle that better.<br /> Absolutely nothing wrong with a dark, deconstructionist superhero game, but it's just not what I want to run right now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T13:49:34-07:00","id":1550760,"title":"","content":"The big thing I'd caution there is that Aberrant 2e is still not a &quot;generic&quot; supers game. There are powers that Novas don't get (or get weaker versions of than you might expect) to leave room for Talents and Psionics for people that want to keep to the original 1e metaplot (at least in broad strokes). If you're telling prospective players &quot;X-men!&quot; a lot, you're running the risk of building up expectations that certain character types that players would gravitate towards might not play out as easily in game; or you might feel like you have to incorporate multiple character types for the players so they can get the mechanics that best suit their character intentions which can be a bit much if you're not used to Storypath or TC at all.<br /> <br /> A simple example here is Domino (esp. since her popularity shot up in general audiences after Deadpool 2). If you want to play Domino in TC... you're going to have the most fun making her a Talent. Trying to make her a Nova to fit in with a team of Novas is probably going to be less fun because Nova powers aren't usually that subtle or indirect. Practically speaking, it's not hard to run a mixed Talent/Nova group (again, something intentional in TC 2e's broader design was to make a game where the Justice League or Avengers are fun to play even when you have exceptional humans like Batman, Green Arrow, Black Widow, and Hawkeye fighting alongside powerhouses like Superman or the Hulk), but it does mean keeping track of two different power types and their individual quirks at the same time. It don't personally think it's that hard to accomplish once you get the hang of it - it's something the games wanted to be functional for groups that want to go that direction, which is most obvious in the relatively small number of mechanical results a power can have and all the powers speak the same 'language' so you can easily handle power interactions - but getting the hang of it isn't something you want to overburden your brain by tossing in too many bits at once.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T14:06:04-07:00","id":1550762,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550760#post1550760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big thing I'd caution there is that Aberrant 2e is still not a &quot;generic&quot; supers game. There are powers that Novas don't get (or get weaker versions of than you might expect) to leave room for Talents and Psionics for people that want to keep to the original 1e metaplot (at least in broad strokes). If you're telling prospective players &quot;X-men!&quot; a lot, you're running the risk of building up expectations that certain character types that players would gravitate towards might not play out as easily in game;</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for the very helpful post.<br /> The X-Men analogy is a loose one. I was refering more to the feel of the game, not so much the actual power level of the characters. I haven't actually announced I'll be running an Aberrant game yet, so I can plan accordingly.<br /> Thanks for the heads up.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T15:39:39-07:00","id":1550767,"title":"","content":"What Heavy Arms is talking about, in part, is that 2e makes a point not to support characters like Professor X or Jean Gray. For &quot;metaplot&quot; reasons, those are reserved for psiads \u2014 a character type that, last time I checked, 2e doesn't support, either. <br /> <br /> With that said, it's not like 1e didn't have its own flaws in that regard. Namely, no Iron Man.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T16:06:33-07:00","id":1550770,"title":"","content":"I was also just avoiding getting into the TC jargon regarding the different types of psi-manipulating expressions. Flux manipulators like Daredevils and Talents are much more consistent across the Continuum in their abilities (and thus rules) to the point where the names are easier to treat as time-period specific terms for the same thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T16:48:34-07:00","id":1550774,"title":"","content":"2e Does support Psiad characters, but you have to have the Aeon book and the Aeon Aexpansion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6-slagheap","name":"Slagheap","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"188"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T10:57:14-07:00","id":1550799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550767#post1550767\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What Heavy Arms is talking about, in part, is that 2e makes a point not to support characters like Professor X or Jean Gray. For &quot;metaplot&quot; reasons, those are reserved for psiads \u2014 a character type that, last time I checked, 2e doesn't support, either.</div> </div> </div> </div>Understood. I can't see that posing much of an issue for my games.<br /> <br /> Out of curiosity, did 1e support telepathy and telekinesis?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T11:03:44-07:00","id":1550801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1550682-aberrant-1e-vs-2e?p=1550770#post1550770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was also just avoiding getting into the TC jargon regarding the different types of psi-manipulating expressions. Flux manipulators like Daredevils and Talents are much more consistent across the Continuum in their abilities (and thus rules) to the point where the names are easier to treat as time-period specific terms for the same thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Appreciated. I'm sure those terms will eventually become as familiar to me as Tal'mahe'ra, Neverborn and Threat Null.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T11:54:42-07:00","id":1550807,"title":"","content":"To address the question you aimed at Dataweaver from my own perspective: kinda. Neither edition of Aberrant has zero support for &quot;psychic&quot; style powers, but it works better if you mechanically take other routes. To keep to the X-Men examples, if you want to make a Nova that lifts people up into the air with a thought, it's easier to go the &quot;Magneto can somehow magnetize the iron in your body and cause you to float,&quot; path than the, &quot;Jean holds her hands up to her head and now you're floating,&quot; path. Even if the end result is basically the same.<br /> <br /> It's easier to think about it from the context that Aberrant bases Nova's core powers on Attributes. So if your character has Mega-Intelligence, they're super-smart. And you can just invest in lots of that, or you can branch out into more specific applications. You can do the Guy Ritchie/RDJ Sherlock Holmes bit where he imagines a fight in his head with such insight that he can make it happen. So it is very easy to do things like Spidey-sense where you can feel attacks coming, and react so quickly, it's indistinguishable from a specific manifestation of precognition. You can speak with such eloquence and poise that it borders on mind control (Mega socials). Etc. etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}